# THIS REPOSITORY OWNS A CEO TODOs.
#
# The presence of this file is the declaration. It switches on
# guard-ceo-todos-commits.sh — the RichOS engine guard that refuses a commit
# while the CEO's TODOs are making a claim they cannot back. Delete it and the TODOs are
# unguarded, which is a visible, reviewable diff and is meant to be.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS
# -------------
# The orchestrator writes long, exact briefs for every teammate: paths,
# commands, constraints, a completion criterion. When the executor is the CEO,
# the brief collapses to one sentence. The most expensive executor in the
# system gets the worst brief, and the same 30-minute task reaches him three
# times with no file, no method and no definition of done.
#
# Underneath that is a structural defect, not a manners problem. A record can
# say an item is "waiting on the CEO" while the thing he is supposed to touch
# has never been prepared and does not exist. That claim is unfalsifiable by
# reading: it looks identical to a real one. So it sits, week after week,
# LOOKING blocked on the most expensive person in the company while it is in
# fact blocked on unfinished preparation by whoever wrote the row.
#
#   AN ITEM MAY NOT CLAIM TO BE WAITING ON THE CEO UNLESS THE THING HE TOUCHES
#   ALREADY EXISTS ON DISK.
#
# THE TWO STATES
# --------------
#   READY-FOR-CEO     prepared: the artifact exists, the time cost is stated,
#                     "done" is written down, and what it unblocks is named.
#                     Only these may sit in a CEO section.
#   BLOCKED-ON-RICH   unprepared: belongs in the preparer's own section.
#
# Moving an item to BLOCKED-ON-RICH is the system working. The CEO sections are
# worth something only while "waiting on the CEO" is a PROMISE that everything
# else is done; one unprepared item sitting there destroys that promise for
# every other item on the page.
#
# PREPARED IS HALF. THE OTHER HALF IS REACHABLE.
# ----------------------------------------------
# The first version of this contract enforced preparation and shipped with
# nowhere to look: the items were buried in a long record and the only new file
# was a dotfile. So there is also exactly ONE entry point — TODO_VIEW — which
# is top-level, un-dotted, named at the head of the root README, and generated
# from the record. It is a projection, never a second copy: the guard refuses a
# view that is not byte-identical to what the record renders to.
#
#   Regenerate:  scripts/ceo-todos-render.sh <this repo>
#   Check:       scripts/ceo-todos-lint.sh   <this repo>
#   Cold-read:   scripts/cold-open.sh --run  <this repo>
#
# (Paths are relative to the RichOS engine. If it is installed by reference,
# that is typically ~/.claude/richos-engine/.)

# The record. One file. The source of truth; the view is derived from it.
TODO_RECORD="@@TODO_RECORD@@"

# The one entry point: a bare TOP-LEVEL file name. Generated — do not
# edit it by hand.
TODO_VIEW="@@TODO_VIEW@@"

# The front door the entry point must be named from, in its first 40 lines.
ROOT_README="README.md"

# Sections that mean "waiting on the CEO". Section @@PREPARER_SECTION@@ is
# where unprepared work goes instead.
CEO_SECTIONS="@@CEO_SECTIONS@@"
PREPARER_SECTION="@@PREPARER_SECTION@@"

# Every artifact path in the record starts with one of these prefixes, so a
# reader always knows which repository a path is in and the checker never has
# to guess between two roots that could both match. Roots are relative to this
# repository's MAIN checkout. A prefix whose root is not on this machine (a
# sibling repo nobody cloned) makes its artifacts UNCHECKABLE and is named in
# every verdict — never a silent pass, and never a block either.
ARTIFACT_ROOTS="@@ARTIFACT_ROOTS@@"

# Where cold-open transcripts live — a reading of this repository's CEO surface
# by someone who is not its builder and has no context. Declaring this key
# switches on the freshness gate: change the front door, and the next commit is
# refused until a fresh cold reading is on file.
#
# The gate checks only that a reading HAPPENED. It has no opinion on what the
# reader concluded, deliberately — a gate that demanded a favorable verdict
# would get one every time.
#
# Leaving it undeclared is allowed and is never a block; every verdict then
# prints that this surface has been read by nobody but the people who built it.
@@COLD_OPEN_DIR@@
